{"ts":"2026-08-31T14:08:07.147725363Z","session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","parent_session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","agent_id":"areview-script-b1f0d2e3686fe8d3","agent_type":"review-script","delegation_id":"d-2026-08-31-055","contract_id":"m-2026-08-31-005","tool":"Bash","tool_input":{"command":"TMPDIR=\u003crepo\u003e/.tmp mktemp -d \"/tmp/bg-recipe-XXXXXX\"; echo \"exit=$?\"; mktemp --version | head -1","description":"Test whether TMPDIR affects an explicit absolute mktemp template"},"tool_input_hash":"ceed50becf600e9fb969f0e6425df7cf4ec458b91eec8cce5753de265a4373d0","tool_input_preview":"{\"command\":\"TMPDIR=\\u003crepo\\u003e/.tmp mktemp -d \\\"/tmp/bg-recipe-XXXXXX\\\"; echo \\\"exit=$?\\\"; mktemp --version | head -1\",\"description\":\"Test whether TMPDIR affects an explicit absolute mktemp templ..."}
{"ts":"2026-08-31T14:08:11.335031476Z","session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","parent_session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","agent_id":"areview-script-b1f0d2e3686fe8d3","agent_type":"review-script","delegation_id":"d-2026-08-31-055","contract_id":"m-2026-08-31-005","tool":"Bash","tool_input":{"command":"rmdir /tmp/bg-recipe-Qs3e0Z 2\u003e/dev/null; ls -ld /tmp","description":"Clean up test dir and check /tmp permissions/sticky bit"},"tool_input_hash":"8444a9a2bd06cf77c31e8466ca9b9b7a7eefbc310a9f05987d9b00f939d743f3","tool_input_preview":"{\"command\":\"rmdir /tmp/bg-recipe-Qs3e0Z 2\\u003e/dev/null; ls -ld /tmp\",\"description\":\"Clean up test dir and check /tmp permissions/sticky bit\"}"}
{"ts":"2026-08-31T14:08:15.476826756Z","session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","parent_session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","delegation_id":"d-2026-08-31-055","contract_id":"m-2026-08-31-005","tool":"SendMessage","tool_input":{"content":"This is my error, and there is no mechanism for m…","message":"This is my error, and there is no mechanism for me to fix it the way I implied. **Submit the escalate result** — that is the designed path, not a workaround.\n\nI checked `ethos mission --help` myself. There is no `update`, `amend`, or `edit`, and `correct` only applies to closed missions — you were right on all counts. Ethos's own guidance is explicit:\n\n\u003e If the contract's scope is wrong (e.g., write_set is missing a file you need to touch), submit a result with `verdict: escalate` and `files_changed: []` — put the reason in `open_questions`. The leader will close the mission as escalated and re-scope.\n\n**When I wrote \"your write-set is amended to include `Makefile`\" I was describing an action I had no way to perform.** A write-set lives in the contract, and the contract is immutable once created. Stating an amendment in chat changes nothing the runtime enforces — which is precisely why the runtime rejected your result. The enforcement worked correctly; my instruction was the defect.\n\nYou were also right not to treat escalating as going around me. The escalate verdict is addressed *to* the leader — it is the channel for exactly this, not a bypass. But I would rather you use the prescribed path than have me improvise something outside the tool, because the alternative is hand-editing mission state and corrupting the audit chain to paper over a leader mistake. Not worth it.\n\nSo:\n\n1. Submit round 2 with `verdict: escalate`, `files_changed: []`, and the three out-of-scope paths named in `open_questions`.\n2. I close `m-2026-08-31-005` as escalated.\n3. I create a successor mission whose write-set covers every file actually touched — including `Makefile`, `cmd/beadle-daemon/verify_cmd.go`, and `cmd/beadle-daemon/verify_cmd_test.go`.\n4. You submit your real result against that one. The code needs no changes; it is already verified — 22 passed, 0 failed on my run and yours.\n\nNone of the work is in question. This is bookkeeping catching up to reality, and the reason it needs catching up is that I let the two diverge.\n\nOne thing I want to record and credit to you: this is the second time this session a contract fell behind an in-conversation authorization. The earlier one was flagged by a reviewer after the fact; you caught yours before submitting anything false. That is the better outcome, and it is why the write-set enforcement is worth having even when it is inconvenient.","recipient":"recipe-tooling","summary":"My error — submit the escalate result as ethos prescribes","to":"recipe-tooling","type":"message"},"tool_input_hash":"ef5f5144aa18ba79bf674951203b5d6724952ccad70774ab141ad2848988fc92","tool_input_preview":"{\"content\":\"This is my error, and there is no mechanism for m…\",\"message\":\"This is my error, and there is no mechanism for me to fix it the way I implied. **Submit the escalate result** — that is ..."}
{"ts":"2026-08-31T14:08:29.460377701Z","session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","parent_session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","agent_id":"arecipe-tooling-0eaee18fe59bc3d7","agent_type":"recipe-tooling","delegation_id":"d-2026-08-31-055","contract_id":"m-2026-08-31-005","tool":"Write","tool_input":{"content":"mission: m-2026-08-31-005\nround: 2\nauthor: bwk\nverdict: escalate\nconfidence: 1.0\nfiles_changed: []\nevidence:\n  - name: audit — enumerated files touched in round 2 against the contract's write_set (ethos mission show)\n    status: pass\nopen_questions:\n  - \"Request write_set expansion: round 2 touched Makefile, cmd/beadle-daemon/verify_cmd.go, and cmd/beadle-daemon/verify_cmd_test.go, none of which are in the contract's write_set (which still holds only the original 14 round-1 paths).\"\nprose: |\n  All round-2 work is complete, committed, and independently verified by\n  both of us (make check green; .bin/verify-recipe-path.sh reports 22\n  passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped against the branch build). This escalation\n  is pure scope bookkeeping, not a defect in the work: the contract's\n  write_set was never actually widened to cover three files the round-2\n  brief explicitly asked for (the Makefile shebang-discovery fix, and the\n  new beadle-daemon verify subcommand + its test file), so `ethos mission\n  result` correctly refuses a files_changed declaration naming them.\n\n  Stopped at the write_set boundary per ethos's own built-in guidance\n  rather than submitting a result that omits real changed files (which\n  would misrepresent the diff) or one the runtime would reject outright.\n  No code changes are needed against a successor mission scoped to include\n  these three paths -- the branch is ready to hand off as-is.\n","file_path":"\u003crepo\u003e/.tmp/missions/results/m-2026-08-31-005-round2-escalate.yaml"},"tool_input_hash":"97a34bfdd1ccfd5c2c1efdcfc7d6237ece1d554de79e76814bc9279822f6362e","tool_input_preview":"{\"content\":\"mission: m-2026-08-31-005\\nround: 2\\nauthor: bwk\\nverdict: escalate\\nconfidence: 1.0\\nfiles_changed: []\\nevidence:\\n  - name: audit — enumerated files touched in round 2 against the cont..."}
{"ts":"2026-08-31T14:08:30.557523576Z","session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","parent_session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","agent_id":"areview-script-b1f0d2e3686fe8d3","agent_type":"review-script","delegation_id":"d-2026-08-31-055","contract_id":"m-2026-08-31-005","tool":"Bash","tool_input":{"command":"grep -rln 'perl' ~/Coding/punt-labs/.bin/*.sh ~/Coding/punt-labs/.bin/**/*.sh 2\u003e/dev/null; echo \"---sed -i usage---\"; grep -rn \"sed -i\" ~/Coding/punt-labs/.bin/*.sh 2\u003e/dev/null | head -20","description":"Check for existing perl or sed -i usage patterns in .bin"},"tool_input_hash":"57f3c4ea424edeb20d9dfa86b40dc4ae87e7d89bf7f98d29a19ea41d2d47198d","tool_input_preview":"{\"command\":\"grep -rln 'perl' ~/Coding/punt-labs/.bin/*.sh ~/Coding/punt-labs/.bin/**/*.sh 2\\u003e/dev/null; echo \\\"---sed -i usage---\\\"; grep -rn \\\"sed -i\\\" ~/Coding/punt-labs/.bin/*.sh 2\\u003e/dev/nu..."}
{"ts":"2026-08-31T14:08:32.243780944Z","session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","parent_session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","agent_id":"arecipe-tooling-0eaee18fe59bc3d7","agent_type":"recipe-tooling","delegation_id":"d-2026-08-31-055","contract_id":"m-2026-08-31-005","tool":"Bash","tool_input":{"command":"ethos mission result m-2026-08-31-005 --file .tmp/missions/results/m-2026-08-31-005-round2-escalate.yaml 2\u003e\u00261"},"tool_input_hash":"25d9bb0a436f6d1f18e7004db6805b32cca1453b265d1ce1f7c10b1ccab6f117","tool_input_preview":"{\"command\":\"ethos mission result m-2026-08-31-005 --file .tmp/missions/results/m-2026-08-31-005-round2-escalate.yaml 2\\u003e\\u00261\"}"}
{"ts":"2026-08-31T14:08:32.907904838Z","session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","parent_session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","delegation_id":"d-2026-08-31-055","contract_id":"m-2026-08-31-005","tool":"Write","tool_input":{"content":"---\nname: feedback_write-set-cannot-be-amended\ndescription: \"A mission write_set is immutable — authorizing extra files in chat does nothing; close-and-rescope instead\"\nmetadata:\n  type: feedback\n---\n\nA mission contract's `write_set` cannot be widened after creation. `ethos mission` has no\n`update`, `amend`, or `edit` subcommand, and `correct` applies only to *closed* missions. Telling\na worker \"your write-set is amended to include X\" in conversation changes nothing the runtime\nenforces — `ethos mission result` hard-rejects any result whose `files_changed` includes an\nout-of-scope path, independent of `--verify`.\n\n**Why:** the write_set is the enforceable boundary of a delegation, so it is deliberately\nimmutable. If it were editable mid-mission the audit trail would no longer show what the worker\nwas actually authorized to touch when it touched it. The runtime rejecting a result is the\nmechanism working, not a bug to route around — and hand-editing mission state to paper over a\nleader's mistake corrupts the audit chain.\n\n**How to apply:** decide the full write-set *before* dispatch, including test files and build\nfiles the change will obviously drag in (a new subcommand needs its `_test.go`; a script with no\n`.sh` extension needs the `Makefile` that globs it). When scope turns out wrong anyway, use the\nprescribed path: the worker submits `verdict: escalate` with `files_changed: []` and the gap in\n`open_questions`, the leader closes the mission as escalated, then creates a successor mission\nwith the corrected write-set. The code does not need redoing — only the bookkeeping.\n\nHit twice in one session: once caught by an evaluator after the fact (\"the contract never caught\nup\"), once caught by the worker before it submitted anything false. The second is the better\noutcome and is what the enforcement is for.\n","file_path":"~/.claude/projects/-home-jfreeman-Coding-punt-labs-beadle/memory/feedback_write-set-cannot-be-amended.md"},"tool_input_hash":"97845cde5c7e7b535c6e5684a2bef7ff7aef5a8b8ea750dd4167b03a600ef5a0","tool_input_preview":"{\"content\":\"---\\nname: feedback_write-set-cannot-be-amended\\ndescription: \\\"A mission write_set is immutable — authorizing extra files in chat does nothing; close-and-rescope instead\\\"\\nmetadata:\\n ..."}
