{"ts":"2026-08-31T14:11:19.307687859Z","session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","tool":"Agent","tool_input":{"description":"Hunt silent failures in recipe tooling","name":"rev-silent","prompt":"Audit `git diff main..HEAD` on `feat/recipe-tooling` in \u003crepo\u003e for silent failures, swallowed errors, and inappropriate fallbacks. 14 files, +1180/-12.\n\n**What it adds.** `beadle-daemon init`/`sign`/`verify` — tooling to author GPG-signed recipe files for the daemon — plus http transport in `MCPServerConfig`, two example recipes, `scripts/beadle-sysreport`, and a `Makefile` shell-script discovery change.\n\n**This repo has a specific history with your failure class, so calibrate to it.** Three separate rounds of fixes this month were exactly your category: guard clauses that abandoned work and logged nothing (`beadle-5k5`), a runner with four paths that truncated or dropped data while reporting success (`beadle-k1g`), and a persistence layer whose `save()` was a silent no-op because the store was never assigned. So the bar here is not \"are errors returned\" but \"does every abandonment leave a trace an operator can act on.\"\n\n**Highest-value targets:**\n\n1. **`sign` and its round-trip verification.** It signs, then calls `VerifySignature` on its own output before writing. If that verification fails, does it fail loudly and leave no partial file behind? A signing tool that writes an unverifiable signature is worse than one that refuses.\n2. **The passphrase path.** `sign` resolves a `gpg-passphrase` credential. `secret.ErrNotFound` is *deliberately* treated as \"empty passphrase, key has none\" — that is intended, not a bug. But check: are other `secret.Get` errors distinguished from not-found, or does any real failure collapse into the same silent empty-passphrase path? That collapse would be a genuine finding.\n3. **`verify`'s exit codes.** It must exit non-zero on anything but a good signature. Check every `SignatureReason` branch reaches a non-zero exit — a reason that falls through to success is exactly the kind of hole that makes a security check decorative.\n4. **`init`.** On an unresolvable authorizer key, does it write nothing, or a partial/empty `daemon.json`? A malformed config disables command loading entirely, so a half-written file is a confusing failure far from its cause.\n5. **`MCPServerConfig` http transport.** Does an unknown or malformed server config fail closed with a named reason, or silently produce a config the worker cannot use?\n6. **`scripts/beadle-sysreport`.** Shell. Does it emit valid JSON on the failure path, or partial output that a `process`-mode stage would happily pass downstream as if it were data?\n\n**Already covered, do not re-verify:** `make check` passes, and `.bin/verify-recipe-path.sh` reports 22/22 including tamper detection and protected-key signing. Those prove the happy paths and a few negatives; they do not prove that failures are legible.\n\nReport each finding with file:line, the concrete scenario that produces the silent failure, and what an operator would see versus what actually happened. Distinguish blocking from polish. If a path looks suspicious but is actually correct with a documented reason, say so — I would rather have a short list of real findings than a long list padded with intended behavior.","subagent_type":"silent-failure-hunter"},"tool_input_hash":"c5c541a86a491b13a12669c7dbb62eedcc1ad66f4a554ec7b90c127441d1ecca","tool_input_preview":"{\"description\":\"Hunt silent failures in recipe tooling\",\"name\":\"rev-silent\",\"prompt\":\"Audit `git diff main..HEAD` on `feat/recipe-tooling` in \\u003crepo\\u003e for silent failures, swallowed errors, an..."}
