{"ts":"2026-08-31T14:16:21.968506965Z","session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","tool":"Bash","tool_input":{"command":"cd ~/Coding/punt-labs; rm -f beadle/beadle-daemon; bd create --title=\"fix(.bin/health): script-safety.py's positional-arg regex matches \\${2:?...} inside optional flag parsing, under-reporting destructive-without-apply\" --type=bug --priority=3 --description=\"FOUND BY: adb, during adversarial review of .bin/verify-recipe-path.sh (2026-08-31).\n\nscript-safety.py's 'destructive-without-apply-or-target' check is satisfied when a script appears to take a required positional argument. Its regex for that also matches a parameter expansion used to validate an OPTIONAL FLAG's value.\n\nConcrete case: .bin/verify-recipe-path.sh contains\n\n    --repo) REPO_DIR=\\\"\\${2:?--repo needs a directory}\\\"; shift 2 ;;\n\n'--repo' is optional and has a default. But \\${2:?...} matches the checker's required-positional pattern, so the script is treated as having a mandatory target and is NOT flagged for performing filesystem writes on bare invocation.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS: the check exists to enforce punt-kit/standards/shell.md's Operational Safety rule -- a script writing outside .tmp/ must default to preview and require --apply/--yes/a positional target. A false negative means the checker silently passes exactly the scripts the rule targets. Any script using \\${N:?...} anywhere in its flag parsing inherits the same blind spot, so this is not specific to one script.\n\nNOT A FALSE ALARM ABOUT THE SCRIPT ITSELF: verify-recipe-path.sh was independently judged not to need --apply -- every mutation is confined to a disposable mktemp sandbox, nothing persists, nothing touches ~/.punt-labs/beadle or the real GnuPG keyring. So the checker reaches the right verdict for that script by the wrong route. The defect is the route.\n\nSCOPE: tighten the regex so it matches an actual positional-argument requirement (e.g. a \\${1:?...} or an explicit arity check outside a case arm for a flag) rather than any \\${N:?...} anywhere in the file. Add a test fixture with an optional flag validated via \\${2:?...} and assert the script IS flagged when it writes outside .tmp/ on bare invocation.\n\nDeliberately filed rather than fixed inline: script-safety.py is shared across every .bin script, so changing its detection could newly flag existing scripts. That triage belongs in its own change, not smuggled into a review of one script.\" 2\u003e\u00261 | tail -2","description":"File the checker false-negative in the workspace repo"},"tool_input_hash":"c1e440ee1f505e3a4b3c369c5a1fc456ebabb85e68be8ff20a46f5d551e0c9f5","tool_input_preview":"{\"command\":\"cd ~/Coding/punt-labs; rm -f beadle/beadle-daemon; bd create --title=\\\"fix(.bin/health): script-safety.py's positional-arg regex matches \\\\${2:?...} inside optional flag parsing, under-rep..."}
