{"ts":"2026-08-31T14:19:21.503734886Z","session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","tool":"Bash","tool_input":{"command":"make build-daemon \u003e/dev/null 2\u003e\u00261; W=~/Coding/punt-labs/.claude/worktrees/adb-verify-recipe; cd \"$W\"; .bin/verify-recipe-path.sh --apply --repo \u003crepo\u003e 2\u003e\u00261 | tail -5; echo \"EXIT: ${PIPESTATUS[0]}\"; python3 .bin/health/script-safety.py .bin/verify-recipe-path.sh \u003e/dev/null 2\u003e\u00261 \u0026\u0026 echo \"safety checker: 0 violations\"","description":"Verify --apply performs the real run"},"tool_input_hash":"174e957ce2bea02d106805a97f4e260e60051598f0e96a1a7d49fcdbebfb2edf","tool_input_preview":"{\"command\":\"make build-daemon \\u003e/dev/null 2\\u003e\\u00261; W=~/Coding/punt-labs/.claude/worktrees/adb-verify-recipe; cd \\\"$W\\\"; .bin/verify-recipe-path.sh --apply --repo \\u003crepo\\u003e 2\\u003e\\u0..."}
{"ts":"2026-08-31T14:19:40.48360854Z","session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","tool":"Bash","tool_input":{"command":"W=~/Coding/punt-labs/.claude/worktrees/adb-verify-recipe; cd \"$W\"; python3 - \u003c\u003c'PY'\np='.bin/README.md'\ns=open(p).read()\nold='| `[--dry-run] [--repo DIR] [--keep]` |'\nnew='| `--apply [--repo DIR] [--keep]` |'\nassert old in s, 'README flags cell not found'\ns=s.replace(old,new,1)\ns=s.replace('destructive (sandbox-scoped: mints real GPG keys',\n            'destructive (previews by default, `--apply` required: mints real GPG keys',1)\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nprint('README updated')\nPY\nnpx --yes markdownlint-cli2 .bin/README.md 2\u003e\u00261 | tail -2; rm -f \u003crepo\u003e/beadle-daemon\ngit add .bin/verify-recipe-path.sh .bin/README.md \u0026\u0026 git commit -q -m \"fix(.bin): preview by default, require --apply\n\nThe operator called out my exemption reasoning, correctly. I had argued\nthat confining every write to a mktemp sandbox gave this a materially\ndifferent risk profile from what punt-kit/standards/shell.md's\npreview-by-default rule targets. That distinction is not in the standard.\nThe rule says any script writing outside .tmp/ previews by default, and\n/tmp/bg-recipe-* is outside .tmp/.\n\nWorse, the reasoning was the exact self-assessment the rule exists to\nremove -- 'safe because the author judges the destination disposable' --\nand the git-pull-all.sh precedent I cited does not hold, since that\nscript is guarded by safety_check, which this one deliberately does not\nuse.\n\nThe script also mints real GPG keys, reads a real secret from pass, and\nsignals gpg-agent. That last one is not sandbox-shaped at all; its safety\nrests entirely on assert_sandbox, a guard I wrote myself.\n\nPreview is now the default and runs BEFORE the preflight hard-fails, so\ndescribing what would happen does not require a built binary. It reports\nunmet prerequisites and what it would mutate. --apply performs the run.\n\nshellcheck clean, script-safety.py 0 violations, --apply run 22/0/0.\" \u0026\u0026 git push 2\u003e\u00261 | tail -1","description":"Update README, commit, push"},"tool_input_hash":"3b7516d18399f8341231396f30eeec119ec8f4a7eac53e7f310e9867cb093cdd","tool_input_preview":"{\"command\":\"W=~/Coding/punt-labs/.claude/worktrees/adb-verify-recipe; cd \\\"$W\\\"; python3 - \\u003c\\u003c'PY'\\np='.bin/README.md'\\ns=open(p).read()\\nold='| `[--dry-run] [--repo DIR] [--keep]` |'\\nnew='|..."}
{"ts":"2026-08-31T14:20:23.109246085Z","session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","tool":"SendMessage","tool_input":{"content":"Truncated mid-fix for finding 1. **Send the rest …","message":"Truncated mid-fix for finding 1. **Send the rest of finding 1's fix, plus findings 2, 3, and 4.**\n\nFirst: finding 1 is the most valuable thing produced on this branch, and possibly this session. Accepted without argument.\n\nYou proved the check is tautological by construction — both sides are `CanonicalCommandBytes(\u0026command)` on one identical value, so `command.Signature = sig` cannot affect it, and the file written at `:112` is a *second* independent marshal the check never reads back. Then you produced a falsifying case with real binaries and a real key: a `prompt` beginning with a newline, where `yaml.v3`'s emitter loses the leading newline, `marshal ∘ unmarshal` is not a fixpoint, and `sign` prints `round-trip verified` while `verify` returns `BADSIG` on the file it just wrote.\n\nProbing ~45 YAML shapes to establish the boundary of the failing family — strings that start with or consist only of newlines — is what turns \"here is a bug\" into \"here is the property that does not hold.\" That distinction is the whole reason I asked for this review instead of trusting the tests.\n\nTwo things you found that I want to make sure are in the record explicitly, because they are worse than the signature problem:\n\n- **The prompt content is silently altered.** The file on disk executes `\"Answer the question.\"`, not what the author wrote. A signing tool that edits the thing it signs is a different and more serious defect than one that miscomputes a digest.\n- **`os.WriteFile` truncates in place**, so this path destroys the operator's authored recipe and replaces it with a file that can never load. `sign_cmd_test.go:156-160` guards the pre-write failure case; there is no guarantee for this path because there is no failure.\n\nI also want to be direct about my own part: I asked you to check exactly this — \"after `sign` writes the signature into the YAML, is the file's canonical form still identical to what was signed?\" — so the question was right. What I then did was accept a harness reporting 22/0/0 as evidence the path worked. My harness greps for `BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE` and never re-derives canonical bytes from the written file, so it was structurally incapable of catching this. Third time this session I have been handed a green signal that could not fail.\n\nFor the remainder, one specific request. On finding 3 (JSON-equivalence versus byte-identity): tell me whether the weaker guarantee is *sufficient* for the actual risk. The reason I demanded byte-identity was that Claude Code parses that config file, and a shape change breaks worker spawning in a way that will not look like an MCP problem. If a JSON-equivalence assertion would still catch that class of regression, say so and I will stop asking for more than the risk warrants. If it would not, tell me what the assertion needs to pin.\n\nSplit across messages rather than truncating again.","recipient":"rev-invariant","summary":"Send remainder — finding 1 is the session's best catch","to":"rev-invariant","type":"message"},"tool_input_hash":"6dba792add2e2a6b5b4753e09b39a9ace22ca3d97ddfe056e1916573d77dce6b","tool_input_preview":"{\"content\":\"Truncated mid-fix for finding 1. **Send the rest …\",\"message\":\"Truncated mid-fix for finding 1. **Send the rest of finding 1's fix, plus findings 2, 3, and 4.**\\n\\nFirst: finding 1 is th..."}
{"ts":"2026-08-31T14:20:45.300785002Z","session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","tool":"SendMessage","tool_input":{"content":"Findings 2 through 5 all accepted. **Send 6 throu…","message":"Findings 2 through 5 all accepted. **Send 6 through 9 plus the two specifics I asked for.**\n\nYour finding 2 is confirmed by an independent line of evidence you could not have had: `rev-invariant`, auditing the same branch, separately established that `sign`'s round-trip check is tautological and produced a real `BADSIG` case. So both of the two things this branch most loudly claims — \"the signature is verified before writing\" and \"context7 grounds the answer\" — turn out to be assertions with no enforcement behind them. Different reviewers, different methods, same shape.\n\nSpecific responses so you know what not to re-argue:\n\n**Finding 2 — accepted as blocking, and your four-point trace is what makes it actionable.** The part I would not have found is point 4: `output_schema: text` makes `CompileSchema` return `(nil, nil)` and `ValidateOutput` a no-op, so the one gate that catches `sysreport`'s malformed output does not exist on `docs-ask`'s path at all. Your framing is exactly right — a failed stage tells the sender nothing happened; this tells the sender something authoritative happened. Take the fail-the-stage fix, and apply the same treatment to `envForCommand`'s identical log-and-continue shape for the `cli` runner. I am not taking the `required_env` contract redesign in this branch; file it as a bead instead.\n\n**Finding 3 — accepted.** Your warning about *where* to validate is the load-bearing part and I want it honored precisely: validate a struct **copy**, because `validateCommand` writes scalar defaults, and `loadCommand` verifies the signature *before* validating, so the canonical bytes must stay pre-default. Signing post-default bytes would manufacture the exact failure the fix is meant to prevent. Also wire `resolveVerifySigner` into `sign` so `--signer` is compared against `daemon.json`'s authorizer, refusing on mismatch unless `--force`, naming both fingerprints.\n\n**Finding 4 — accepted.** Probe the key with `pgp.CheckKeyExpiry` before writing, with `--no-verify-key` as the explicit escape hatch. Noting for the record that you answered the *opposite* of what my brief asked — I asked whether `init` writes a partial file, and you established it does not and that the ordering and its guard comment are sound. Correcting a premise in my brief is more useful than answering it.\n\n**Finding 5 — accepted, and it is the one with the widest blast radius.** `secret.go:36` discarding every keychain error affects every `secret.Get` caller in the repo, not just `sign`. Thread the discarded error into the final `ErrNotFound` wrap. You are right that `sign_cmd.go:90` handles its half correctly and should be left alone — the message is good, the input is wrong.\n\n**My two specifics, still wanted:** whether the re-marshal-over-source problem appears anywhere else in the diff or is confined to `sign_cmd.go`; and your read on the `os.WriteFile` truncation as independently blocking (I think it is — `internal/daemon/persist.go` already does temp-plus-rename in this same package, so there is an in-repo pattern to copy rather than only a standard to cite).\n\nOne thing you should know about the harness that reported 22/0/0 on all of this: it greps for `BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE` and never checks that the rest of the file survived, nor re-derives canonical bytes from what was written. I wrote it. It is green and structurally incapable of catching finding 1 or `rev-invariant`'s finding 1. If you see other assertions in this branch's tests with that shape — passing, and unable to fail — flag them in your remaining findings even if they are outside the silent-failure remit.","recipient":"rev-silent","summary":"Findings 2-5 accepted; send 6-9","to":"rev-silent","type":"message"},"tool_input_hash":"9dc95309a0ea44149f26811d82a1c2690fbcbb1ad4a3caa95c58a4e4e6a28dd2","tool_input_preview":"{\"content\":\"Findings 2 through 5 all accepted. **Send 6 throu…\",\"message\":\"Findings 2 through 5 all accepted. **Send 6 through 9 plus the two specifics I asked for.**\\n\\nYour finding 2 is confirmed ..."}
