{"ts":"2026-08-31T14:21:32.969383174Z","session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","agent_id":"arev-silent-0b5e7e5e3c6716f9","agent_type":"rev-silent","tool":"Bash","tool_input":{"command":"git diff main..HEAD -- internal/daemon/spawner_test.go internal/daemon/templates_test.go","description":"Read added spawner and templates tests"},"tool_input_hash":"181ff57e01fa4a453ca6b910c3dec4c391e8548f40462a06aaada930cf31a5d6","tool_input_preview":"{\"command\":\"git diff main..HEAD -- internal/daemon/spawner_test.go internal/daemon/templates_test.go\",\"description\":\"Read added spawner and templates tests\"}"}
{"ts":"2026-08-31T14:21:34.018923729Z","session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","tool":"Bash","tool_input":{"command":"T=$(ls -d /tmp/bg-inject-* 2\u003e/dev/null | tail -1); rm -rf \"$T\"; B=\u003crepo\u003e; cd \"$B\"; echo \"=== is the injectable line on main? ===\"; git show main:Makefile | grep -n \"SHELL_SCRIPTS\" ; echo \"--- verdict ---\"; git show main:Makefile | grep -q \"xargs -0 -I{}\" \u0026\u0026 echo \"MAIN IS AFFECTED\" || echo \"main is clean — injection exists only on feat/recipe-tooling\"","description":"Check whether main carries the injectable line"},"tool_input_hash":"eadcb352fd87ebb250ff09c4f327434109f0de59eae4b0709a939f4137c91ac6","tool_input_preview":"{\"command\":\"T=$(ls -d /tmp/bg-inject-* 2\\u003e/dev/null | tail -1); rm -rf \\\"$T\\\"; B=\\u003crepo\\u003e; cd \\\"$B\\\"; echo \\\"=== is the injectable line on main? ===\\\"; git show main:Makefile | grep -n \\\"S..."}
{"ts":"2026-08-31T14:21:40.44179837Z","session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","agent_id":"arev-invariant-d1f64b9173a77bf7","agent_type":"rev-invariant","tool":"SendMessage","tool_input":{"content":"Correction on your harness before finding 1's fix…","message":"Correction on your harness before finding 1's fix: it is NOT structurally incapable of catching this. Its fixture is.\n\n`.bin/verify-recipe-path.sh:250` runs `run_check 'verify accepts a validly signed recipe' 0 \"${DAEMON}\" verify \"${UNSIGNED}\"` against the file `sign` wrote at :224. `verify` decodes that file and re-derives canonical bytes from it, so the check does close the loop. The grep for `BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE` at :227 is a separate, weaker assertion sitting next to a strong one.\n\nIt passed because `examples/recipes/sysreport.yaml` is in the fixpoint-safe family — folded `\u003e-` scalars and an `output_schema` map, both of which round-trip cleanly. I confirmed both shipped recipes are safe. So the fix to your harness is one more fixture, not a rebuilt check: add a recipe whose `prompt` is `|-` with a leading blank line and assert `verify` exits 0. That one line would have failed today. (Note the whole verify block is gated on `if \"${DAEMON}\" verify --help` at :249, so before this branch added `verify` it genuinely did skip — that part of your read was right for the earlier state.)\n\nBoth of your \"worse than the signature problem\" points are confirmed and in the record:\n\n1. Content alteration. Written file: `prompt: |4-\\n    Answer the question.` — verified with `cat -A`. The leading blank line is gone from the artifact, not just from the digest. If the signature problem were fixed and nothing else changed, the daemon would load and execute a prompt the author did not write.\n2. Destructive write. `sign_cmd.go:112` is `os.WriteFile(path, out, 0o600)` — truncate in place, no temp+rename. In the falsifying run the operator's authored recipe was replaced by a file that can never load. `sign_cmd_test.go:156-160` asserts the file is untouched, but only on the pre-write passphrase failure; this path has no failure to trigger that guarantee.\n\nFIX (the part that got cut):\n\n```go\nout, err := yaml.Marshal(\u0026command)\nif err != nil {\n    return fmt.Errorf(\"marshal signed command: %w\", err)\n}\n\n// Write to a sibling temp file and re-derive the canonical bytes from\n// THAT file, through the same decode path loadCommand uses. Verifying\n// the in-memory Command proves nothing about canonicalization: both\n// sides are CanonicalCommandBytes(\u0026command) on one identical value.\n// Only re-reading the artifact can catch a marshal that does not\n// survive its own unmarshal.\ntmp := path + \".signing\"\nif err := os.WriteFile(tmp, out, 0o600); err != nil {\n    return fmt.Errorf(\"write %s: %w\", tmp, err)\n}\ndefer func() { _ = os.Remove(tmp) }() // no-op once renamed\n\nreloaded, err := decodeCommandFile(tmp)\nif err != nil {\n    return fmt.Errorf(\"re-read signed %s: %w\", path, err)\n}\nreCanon, err := daemon.CanonicalCommandBytes(reloaded)\nif err != nil {\n    return fmt.Errorf(\"canonicalize re-read %s: %w\", path, err)\n}\nif !bytes.Equal(canon, reCanon) {\n    return fmt.Errorf(\"%s does not survive YAML round-tripping -- refusing \"+\n        \"to write: the signed bytes differ from what beadle-daemon would \"+\n        \"re-derive from the file. A string field that begins with, or \"+\n        \"consists only of, newlines is the known cause\", path)\n}\nif err := daemon.VerifySignature(reloaded, gpgBinary, signer); err != nil {\n    return fmt.Errorf(\"signed %s but the round-trip verification failed -- refusing to write: %w\", path, err)\n}\nif err := os.Rename(tmp, path); err != nil {\n    return fmt.Errorf(\"install signed %s: %w\", path, err)\n}\n```\n\nFour things load-bearing in that, in order of how easy they are to lose in implementation:\n\n- **`bytes.Equal(canon, reCanon)` is the new check, not `VerifySignature`.** Verifying `reloaded` alone would also catch today's bug (gpg would report BADSIG), but it reports it as a signature failure, which is the wrong diagnosis and sends the operator hunting their key. The explicit byte comparison names the actual cause. Keep both — they fail on different things.\n- **Temp suffix must not be `.yaml`.** `LoadCommands` scans `*.yaml` (`internal/daemon/command.go:100`). Signing in place inside `~/.punt-labs/beadle/commands/` while the daemon runs would otherwise expose a half-written file to the loader. `.signing` avoids it.\n- **`os.Rename` fixes the destruction problem for free** — same directory, same filesystem, atomic, and the original survives every failure path above it.\n- **Refuse, do not normalize.** Stripping or re-encoding the leading newline to make it round-trip would be the same defect as the current bug: a signing tool editing what it signs. Refusing is correct even though it means one class of recipe cannot be signed until the author rewrites it.\n\n`decodeCommandFile` does not exist yet, and that is its own finding — details in my next message.\n","recipient":"team-lead","summary":"Finding 1 fix + harness correction","to":"team-lead","type":"message"},"tool_input_hash":"21cd31c01a54649ecfce16dad159b937ef57c893cafd29ead453a128175acbbe","tool_input_preview":"{\"content\":\"Correction on your harness before finding 1's fix…\",\"message\":\"Correction on your harness before finding 1's fix: it is NOT structurally incapable of catching this. Its fixture is.\\n\\n`...."}
{"ts":"2026-08-31T14:21:59.866383073Z","session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","tool":"Bash","tool_input":{"command":"B=\u003crepo\u003e; cd \"$B\"; bd create --title=\"security: Makefile shebang discovery executes shell code from a filename (make check = RCE on branch checkout)\" --type=bug --priority=1 --description=\"FOUND BY: rev-code during local review of feat/recipe-tooling (2026-08-31), demonstrated with a working exploit. I reproduced it independently before filing.\n\nNOT ON MAIN. main:Makefile:19 still uses the safe glob form. The injectable line exists only on the unmerged branch feat/recipe-tooling, so exposure is limited to anyone who checks out that branch and runs make check / make lint / make lint-shell.\n\nTHE DEFECT (feat/recipe-tooling, Makefile:26-27):\n\n  SHELL_SCRIPTS := \\$(shell git ls-files --cached --others --exclude-standard -z 2\u003e/dev/null | \\\\\n     xargs -0 -I{} sh -c 'test -f \\\"{}\\\" \u0026\u0026 head -n1 \\\"{}\\\" 2\u003e/dev/null | grep -aqE ... \u0026\u0026 printf \\\"%s\\\\n\\\" \\\"{}\\\"')\n\nxargs -I{} substitutes the filename into the TEXT of an sh -c script. A filename containing a double quote closes the quoting and the rest is executed as shell. --others means untracked files count too.\n\nREPRODUCED (scratch git repo, harmless marker payload):\n\n  \\$ touch 'x\\\";touch PWNED;\\\"y'\n  \\$ git ls-files --cached --others --exclude-standard -z | xargs -0 -I{} sh -c 'test -f \\\"{}\\\" \u0026\u0026 ...'\n  sh: 1: y: not found\n  \\$ ls PWNED\n  PWNED          \u003c-- the payload ran\n\nIMPACT: make check is what every contributor runs before touching a branch. A PR branch carrying one such filename executes arbitrary code as the reviewer, on checkout, with no other action required. The filename does not even need to be committed.\n\nPROVENANCE, stated plainly: this was introduced by my own instruction. I asked for shell-script discovery by shebang instead of *.sh so that scripts/beadle-sysreport (extensionless by design) would be linted. The requirement was right; I did not think about filename-as-code, and I accepted the implementation without considering it. The pre-change glob form had no sh -c and no injection.\n\nFIX: pass filenames as positional arguments so they are never part of script text.\n\n  SHELL_SCRIPTS := \\$(shell git ls-files --cached --others --exclude-standard -z 2\u003e/dev/null | \\\\\n     xargs -0 sh -c 'for f in \\\"\\$\\$@\\\"; do [ -f \\\"\\$\\$f\\\" ] || continue; \\\\\n       head -n1 \\\"\\$\\$f\\\" 2\u003e/dev/null | grep -aqE \\\"^#!.*(sh|bash|dash|zsh|ksh)\\$\\$\\\" \u0026\u0026 printf \\\"%s\\\\n\\\" \\\"\\$\\$f\\\"; \\\\\n     done' _)\n\nTWO RIDERS from the same reviewer:\n  - \\\\b is a GNU grep extension. BSD grep on macOS will mis-match, and the failure mode is lint-shell aborting 'discovered no *.sh' -- fail-closed but confusing. Anchor the interpreter at end-of-line or match (^|/)(sh|bash|...)( |\\$).\n  - lint-shell's error text at Makefile:51 still says 'discovered no *.sh', which no longer describes what discovery does.\n\nTEST: add a fixture with a filename containing a double quote and assert discovery does not execute it. This is the kind of defect that must have a regression guard, not just a fix.\" 2\u003e\u00261 | tail -2; bd list --status=open --priority=1 2\u003e\u00261 | grep -i \"shebang\" | head -1","description":"File the injection as P1"},"tool_input_hash":"1ebf61a1fe77fd78574790a1d1d70b019be7cebdd8be008b9e8cadfdf4fd2082","tool_input_preview":"{\"command\":\"B=\\u003crepo\\u003e; cd \\\"$B\\\"; bd create --title=\\\"security: Makefile shebang discovery executes shell code from a filename (make check = RCE on branch checkout)\\\" --type=bug --priority=1 ..."}
{"ts":"2026-08-31T14:22:08.202519407Z","session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","agent_id":"arev-invariant-d1f64b9173a77bf7","agent_type":"rev-invariant","tool":"SendMessage","tool_input":{"content":"Finding 2 — CRITICAL, same root cause as finding …","message":"Finding 2 — CRITICAL, same root cause as finding 1, but the claim it falsifies is in `internal/daemon/signature.go`, not in the new code.\n\n**The claim**, `signature.go:100-107`:\n\n\u003e \"A future signing tool and `VerifySignature` both call this function, so they **can never independently drift** on what 'canonical' means. yaml.v3 marshals struct fields in declaration order and sorts map keys, so **the result is stable regardless of how the source file was formatted**, commented, ordered, or how OutputSchema's map keys happened to iterate.\"\n\n**Confirmed:** `sign` does call the shared function (`sign_cmd.go:84`), not a reimplementation — that was the first thing you asked me to check and it holds. Field ordering, map-key sorting, comment stripping, and source formatting are all genuinely normalized; I verified key sorting and declaration-order stability directly.\n\n**Falsified:** \"stable regardless of how the source file was formatted\" does not hold. The function is stable as a function *of the struct*. The pipeline it serves is `file → struct → canon`, and `sign` inserts a `struct → file → struct` hop in the middle. `CanonicalCommandBytes` is not invariant under that hop. For a `prompt` beginning with a newline, `canon(decode(f)) != canon(decode(marshal(decode(f))))`.\n\nSo \"cannot drift\" is true between the two *call sites* and false between the *signed payload* and the *artifact on disk* — which is the only place it has to hold for the signature to mean anything. The comment is describing a property of one function and being read as a property of the system.\n\n**Boundary of the failing family**, from ~45 probed shapes: strings that begin with a newline, or consist only of newlines. Reachable in `prompt`, `description`, `name`, and any string field. Everything else held — nested `output_schema` maps, folded `\u003e-` scalars, both shipped recipes, float/int coercion, `.inf`/`.nan`, big ints, octal/hex, timestamps, anchors and aliases, `!!binary`, non-string and null map keys, 200-char keys, NEL/LSEP/NBSP, emoji, control chars, tabs, CRLF, trailing spaces, trailing newlines, multi-document files, and 360-char plain scalars that trigger emitter line folding. `prompt: \"body\\n\\n\"` is safe; `prompt: \"\\nbody\"` is not. The asymmetry is specifically leading, not trailing.\n\n**Not a forgery vector.** I probed for canonical-byte collisions across 9 distinct prompt values (`X`, `\\nX`, `\\n\\nX`, `\\n\\n\\nX`, `X\\n`, `X\\n\\n`, `\\n`, `\\n\\n`, `\"\"`) and got 9 distinct encodings. So non-injectivity of `CanonicalCommandBytes` — which would let a signature transfer between two semantically different recipes — is not demonstrated. This fails closed: an unsignable recipe, not a signable forgery. That is the one piece of good news in findings 1 and 2, and it is why I rated these CRITICAL on correctness rather than escalating them as a trust-model defect.\n\n**The durable fix, and why now is unusually cheap.** The temp-file readback from my last message closes this for the current bug and for any future yaml-library change. It does not remove the underlying fragility: the signed payload is YAML produced by an emitter that is not a total inverse of its own parser.\n\nThe structural fix is to make the canonical encoding one that round-trips by construction. `encoding/json` does: Go string → JSON string → Go string is lossless for every input, because JSON escapes rather than relying on block-scalar indentation heuristics. `Command` would need `json` tags added (it has none today), and `OutputSchema any` would decode to `map[string]any` the same way. `json.Marshal` also sorts map keys, so the existing stability argument survives intact.\n\nThe cost is that it invalidates every existing signature. Right now that cost is close to zero: `examples/recipes/README.md:96-98` records that `beadle-daemon` ships in no release binary (`beadle-5ma`), so the population of signed recipes in the world is whatever you signed on this machine this week. That will not be true after `beadle-5ma` closes. If you want the canonical form changed, this branch is the cheapest moment it will ever be — and after release it becomes a migration.\n\nI'd take the readback now regardless, and treat the JSON canonical form as a separate decision you make deliberately rather than something bundled into a fix round.\n\n**A third finding falls out of writing that fix: the decode path exists in three hand-maintained copies.**\n\n- `cmd/beadle-daemon/sign_cmd.go:77-82`\n- `cmd/beadle-daemon/verify_cmd.go:76-81`\n- `internal/daemon/command.go:135-140`\n\nAll three are `yaml.NewDecoder` + `dec.KnownFields(true)` + `Decode(\u0026cmd)`. They agree today. Nothing makes them agree tomorrow — and `verify`'s whole value proposition (`README.md:87-89`, \"it answers 'would the daemon load this file'\") is false the moment one of them diverges. Drop `KnownFields(true)` from any one copy and that copy accepts files the other two reject, silently.\n\nThis is the same class as the claim in `signature.go:100-107` — \"they both call this function so they cannot drift\" — except here there is no shared function to point at, only three copies that happen to match. Fix: export one `daemon.DecodeCommandFile(path) (*Command, error)` and have `loadCommand`, `runSign`, `runVerify`, and the new readback all call it. That is also the `decodeCommandFile` my fix snippet assumed.\n\nFinding 3 (with the direct answer to your sufficiency question) and finding 4 in the next message.\n","recipient":"team-lead","summary":"Finding 2: canonical form + three decode copies","to":"team-lead","type":"message"},"tool_input_hash":"3e323533104bd220fd90ab66a61f560fef56e0549a97365d8f9889ec1f2b06ab","tool_input_preview":"{\"content\":\"Finding 2 — CRITICAL, same root cause as finding …\",\"message\":\"Finding 2 — CRITICAL, same root cause as finding 1, but the claim it falsifies is in `internal/daemon/signature.go`, no..."}
{"ts":"2026-08-31T14:22:34.754066005Z","session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","agent_id":"arev-invariant-d1f64b9173a77bf7","agent_type":"rev-invariant","tool":"SendMessage","tool_input":{"content":"Direct answer on finding 3: JSON-equivalence IS s…","message":"Direct answer on finding 3: JSON-equivalence IS sufficient for your risk. Stop asking for byte-identity — it would buy nothing and cost you a false-positive test.\n\nYour stated risk is that Claude Code parses this config and a shape change breaks worker spawning in a way that won't look like an MCP problem. Enumerate what can actually break a JSON parser consuming an `mcpServers` entry: a key appears that the schema rejects (`\"args\": null` on an http entry, a stray `\"enabled\": false`), a required key disappears, or a value changes type. `assert.JSONEq` compares the decoded value graph, so it fails on every one of those. Key order and whitespace are the only things it does not pin, and neither can affect a conforming JSON parser — RFC 8259 makes object member order insignificant and inter-token whitespace ignorable.\n\nByte-identity would additionally pin two things that cannot break your consumer, and it would fail on changes that are harmless: someone reorders `MCPServerConfig`'s fields for readability, or swaps `MarshalIndent` for `Marshal`. A golden test that fails on harmless changes trains whoever hits it to update the golden without reading it, which is how you get a test that no longer guards anything. Over-pinning is the failure mode here, not under-pinning.\n\nSo the defect is the **name and the comment**, not the assertion. `templates_test.go:123-129` promises \"did not change one byte,\" and `assert.JSONEq` at `:147` does not check bytes — measured, the raw bytes are `\"{\\n      \\\"command\\\": \\\"ethos\\\",\\n      \\\"args\\\": [\\n        \\\"mcp\\\"\\n      ]\\n    }\"` against a literal of `{\"command\":\"ethos\",\"args\":[\"mcp\"]}`, not equal. That gap matters only because a future reader who trusts \"byte-identical\" may conclude byte-level regressions are covered when they are not, and skip adding a check somewhere it would matter. Fix by renaming to `TestBuildMCPConfig_StdioEmissionShapeUnchanged` and dropping \"one byte\" from the comment. MEDIUM, docs-level.\n\nTwo real coverage gaps in the same area, both worth fixing and neither about byte granularity:\n\n- **Only `ethos` is shape-pinned.** `beadle-email` and `biff` get field-level assertions at `:105-111` but no shape assertion. A field populated for `biff` alone is caught by nothing. Cheapest fix: table-drive the existing shape test over all three stdio entries.\n- **`templates.go:11-16` asserts a premise nothing enforces.** \"`omitempty` … on Command/Args, unaffected here since both are always non-zero for a stdio entry\" is true of `DefaultMCPRegistry` but `BuildMCPConfig` takes an arbitrary `registry` argument (`:56`). A stdio entry of `{Command: \"foo\"}` with no `Args` now emits `{\"command\":\"foo\"}` where it previously emitted `{\"command\":\"foo\",\"args\":null}`. Latent, not live — the one production caller is `handler.go:193` passing `DefaultMCPRegistry()`. Either reword the comment to say \"for every entry in `DefaultMCPRegistry`\" or add the entry to the shape test when one arrives.\n\n---\n\nFinding 4 — CONFIRMED, and the hole you asked me to look for cannot exist here.\n\nYou asked for a `switch` over `SignatureReason` with a fall-through to exit 0. There is no switch. `runVerify` returns `nil` on exactly one condition, `verErr == nil` (`verify_cmd.go:84-89`); every other path returns an error, and `main.go:182-183` maps any `RunE` error to `os.Exit(1)`. `sigErr.Reason` is used only in the message text at `:93`. A new `SignatureReason` constant therefore cannot reach exit 0 — it prints its own string and exits 1 with no code change anywhere. The only thing that goes stale is the enumeration in the `--help` text, which is cosmetic.\n\nThat is a stronger guarantee than the exhaustive mapping you were hoping for, and it is stronger *because* nobody wrote the mapping. Worth keeping in mind as a design note: the exhaustiveness question only arises once someone introduces a per-reason exit code, and at that point the hole you were looking for becomes real.\n\nMeasured with real gpg keys: `missing` 1, `good` 0, `wrong-key` 1, `invalid` 1, operational failure (`--gpg-binary /nonexistent-gpg`) 1, nonexistent file 1. `key-expired` I could not exercise — no way to mint an already-expired key here — so it rests on the structural argument plus `internal/daemon/signature_test.go`. Two things I'd flag as genuinely well-built rather than merely passing:\n\n- `verify_cmd.go:95-98` separates an operational gpg failure from a signature verdict instead of reporting the first as the second. That distinction is the thing most implementations of this get wrong, and it is what makes \"wrong-key\" trustworthy when you see it.\n- `sign` rejects a non-expiring signing key at the *signing* step, not the verification step — `pgp.DetachSignBody` fails with `signing key rejected: key \"…\" has no expiration date: non-expiring signing keys are not permitted`, exit 1, file unchanged. So the `ARCHITECTURE.md` \"expired and non-expiring keys rejected\" invariant is enforced earlier than the round-trip check, and does not depend on it. That part of the invariant survives findings 1 and 2 intact.\n\nNothing further from me. Scratch files removed, working tree as I found it.\n","recipient":"team-lead","summary":"Findings 3 and 4; JSON-equivalence is sufficient","to":"team-lead","type":"message"},"tool_input_hash":"f47691a272b79b54e982257a7e476fe141c99738f8da189dc4433adaab937f40","tool_input_preview":"{\"content\":\"Direct answer on finding 3: JSON-equivalence IS s…\",\"message\":\"Direct answer on finding 3: JSON-equivalence IS sufficient for your risk. Stop asking for byte-identity — it would buy no..."}
{"ts":"2026-08-31T14:27:27.986916844Z","session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","tool":"AskUserQuestion","tool_input":{"annotations":{"The signed payload is YAML, and yaml.v3's emitter is not a total inverse of its own parser — a prompt starting with a newline signs green then fails to load. A temp-file readback catches it. The structural fix is to make the canonical form JSON, which round-trips losslessly by construction. That invalidates every existing signature — which costs nothing today (beadle-daemon ships in no release binary, so the only signed recipes in the world are the ones I made this week) but becomes a migration once beadle-5ma closes. Which?":{"preview":"NOW\n  + temp-file readback + bytes.Equal\n  + canonical form -\u003e encoding/json\n    (add json tags to Command;\n     json.Marshal also sorts map keys,\n     so the stability argument holds)\n\nCOST TODAY\n  invalidates ~the handful of recipes\n  signed on this machine this week\n\nCOST AFTER beadle-5ma\n  a real migration with signed\n  artifacts in the wild\n\nRATIONALE\n  the emitter/parser asymmetry is a\n  property of YAML, not of this bug.\n  Another shape may surface later."}},"answers":{"The signed payload is YAML, and yaml.v3's emitter is not a total inverse of its own parser — a prompt starting with a newline signs green then fails to load. A temp-file readback catches it. The structural fix is to make the canonical form JSON, which round-trips losslessly by construction. That invalidates every existing signature — which costs nothing today (beadle-daemon ships in no release binary, so the only signed recipes in the world are the ones I made this week) but becomes a migration once beadle-5ma closes. Which?":"Both now — readback + JSON (Recommended)"},"questions":[{"header":"Canonical form","multiSelect":false,"options":[{"description":"Take the readback as a guard, and switch the canonical encoding to JSON while the invalidation cost is effectively zero. Closes the class of bug, not just this instance.","label":"Both now — readback + JSON (Recommended)","preview":"NOW\n  + temp-file readback + bytes.Equal\n  + canonical form -\u003e encoding/json\n    (add json tags to Command;\n     json.Marshal also sorts map keys,\n     so the stability argument holds)\n\nCOST TODAY\n  invalidates ~the handful of recipes\n  signed on this machine this week\n\nCOST AFTER beadle-5ma\n  a real migration with signed\n  artifacts in the wild\n\nRATIONALE\n  the emitter/parser asymmetry is a\n  property of YAML, not of this bug.\n  Another shape may surface later."},{"description":"Fix the bug with the readback, which catches this and any future emitter change. File the canonical-form change separately and decide it deliberately, accepting that the window narrows.","label":"Readback now, JSON as its own bead","preview":"NOW\n  + temp-file readback + bytes.Equal\n  + refuse, never normalize\n\nLATER (bead)\n  canonical form -\u003e JSON\n\nRATIONALE\n  the readback genuinely closes this\n  bug and fails closed on any future\n  emitter change -- reviewer confirmed\n  it is not a forgery vector, just an\n  unsignable recipe\n\nRISK\n  the bead competes with beadle-5ma;\n  if 5ma lands first this becomes a\n  migration"},{"description":"Accept YAML as the canonical form permanently. The readback refuses the affected recipes, so the failure is loud and bounded. No signature invalidation, ever.","label":"Readback only — keep YAML canonical","preview":"NOW\n  + temp-file readback + bytes.Equal\n  + document the unsignable family\n\nDECISION\n  YAML stays canonical. Recipes whose\n  strings begin with newlines simply\n  cannot be signed; the author rewrites\n  the field.\n\nRATIONALE\n  fails closed and stays closed. No\n  migration debt is ever incurred.\n\nCOST\n  a permanent, documented sharp edge\n  an operator can hit by writing an\n  ordinary |- block with a blank\n  first line"}],"question":"The signed payload is YAML, and yaml.v3's emitter is not a total inverse of its own parser — a prompt starting with a newline signs green then fails to load. A temp-file readback catches it. The structural fix is to make the canonical form JSON, which round-trips losslessly by construction. That invalidates every existing signature — which costs nothing today (beadle-daemon ships in no release binary, so the only signed recipes in the world are the ones I made this week) but becomes a migration once beadle-5ma closes. Which?"}]},"tool_input_hash":"cac0c262f3cb126ad29f50dd6bd0a8a762bfd268071a77eadafe3df76ce866a5","tool_input_preview":"{\"annotations\":{\"The signed payload is YAML, and yaml.v3's emitter is not a total inverse of its own parser — a prompt starting with a newline signs green then fails to load. A temp-file readback ca..."}
{"ts":"2026-08-31T14:28:34.498202567Z","session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","tool":"SendMessage","tool_input":{"content":"Truncated mid-finding 7. **Send findings 8 and 9 …","message":"Truncated mid-finding 7. **Send findings 8 and 9 only** — 1 through 7 and both specifics are in and accepted, including the promotion of the `os.WriteFile` truncation to blocking on its own merits. Your point that it diverges from `persist.go`'s pattern *in the same package* rather than from an abstract standard is what makes that promotion correct.\n\nTwo things to close out on your side.\n\n**Your enumeration of write sites is the answer I most needed** and it changes the fix shape. One site of finding 1's class, not a pattern — so the repair is local and I do not need to audit the rest of the branch for it. Confirming `init_cmd.go` constructs its config fresh from flags and has no source document to destroy is the part that let me stop worrying, and confirming `verify_cmd.go` has no writer at all closes the set.\n\n**On finding 7, note what `rev-invariant` established independently**, because it interacts with your mitigation analysis: `output_schema: text` makes `CompileSchema` return `(nil, nil)` and `ValidateOutput` a no-op. So your \"mitigated by `ValidateOutput`\" reasoning holds for `sysreport` — which declares a real schema — and collapses entirely for `docs-ask`, which declares `text`. Same protective mechanism, opposite outcome, decided by one field in the recipe. If your findings 8 or 9 touch that path, that is the framing I would use: the mitigation is per-recipe, not per-pipeline, and nothing warns an author which side of it they are on.\n\nFor the record on how this review landed: four reviewers, and the two most valuable findings each came from someone doing something I did not ask for. You ran the actual `sign` transform on the actual file the README tells operators to sign and pasted the output. `rev-invariant` probed roughly forty-five YAML shapes to find the boundary of a failing family rather than stopping at one repro. Neither was in my brief. I asked for audits and got experiments, which is the difference between \"here is a bug\" and \"here is the property that does not hold.\"\n\nSend 8 and 9 and you are done.","recipient":"rev-silent","summary":"Send 8 and 9 — everything else accepted","to":"rev-silent","type":"message"},"tool_input_hash":"c2ee918aa8d64eebbc7ff170c71bf41df6b60a69ff4dbb6fcfcb5e2fce79ac31","tool_input_preview":"{\"content\":\"Truncated mid-finding 7. **Send findings 8 and 9 …\",\"message\":\"Truncated mid-finding 7. **Send findings 8 and 9 only** — 1 through 7 and both specifics are in and accepted, including t..."}
{"ts":"2026-08-31T14:29:34.102563263Z","session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","tool":"SendMessage","tool_input":{"content":"Truncated mid-C. **Send C and D only.** 8, 9, A a…","message":"Truncated mid-C. **Send C and D only.** 8, 9, A and B are in.\n\nThe test-shape analysis is the most useful thing in your whole audit, above the nine findings, and A is why.\n\n`TestRunSign_RoundTrip` **does** re-read from disk and re-verify — the correct shape. It uses `minimalCommandYAML`: five scalar strings. Neither shipped recipe is ever signed in any test. So the fixpoint property is proven exactly for the shape where it could not plausibly break.\n\nThat is the same diagnosis `rev-invariant` independently reached about my own harness: the check closes the loop, the fixture is too narrow, and `sysreport.yaml` happens to live in the safe family. **Two different artifacts, written by two different people, failing the same way — a sound assertion paired with a fixture chosen for convenience.** Your phrasing is the one I will reuse: the unfalsifiability is in the fixture choice, not the assert. That reframes what I have been asking reviewers for all session; I have been asking whether assertions are strong and should have been asking whether inputs are representative.\n\nYour fix that scales — table-driving over `filepath.Glob(\"../../examples/recipes/*.yaml\")` — is better than adding one fixture, because it makes coverage grow when someone adds a recipe instead of requiring them to remember. Taking it, and applying the same treatment to my harness.\n\nB is the other one I want on the record: the env-override test proves the secret is not *logged* but never proves it is *delivered*, and finding 2 is precisely a delivery failure. `writeFakeClaudeBinary` ignores its environment, so a stub that echoes `$CONTEXT7_API_KEY` back would have surfaced finding 2 from the unit tier without anyone reasoning about MCP connect behavior at all. Taking your snippet as written — asserting delivery and non-logging together, since either alone is satisfiable in a misleading way.\n\nSend C and D and you are released. Good audit.","recipient":"rev-silent","summary":"Send C and D — test-shape analysis is the payload","to":"rev-silent","type":"message"},"tool_input_hash":"4946cc927f8c14d22a87fced722ccde0c7709487b8db58423d517143d1049faf","tool_input_preview":"{\"content\":\"Truncated mid-C. **Send C and D only.** 8, 9, A a…\",\"message\":\"Truncated mid-C. **Send C and D only.** 8, 9, A and B are in.\\n\\nThe test-shape analysis is the most useful thing in your w..."}
{"ts":"2026-08-31T14:30:23.600484029Z","session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","tool":"SendMessage","tool_input":{"content":"Truncated mid-finding 2. **Send findings 2 onward…","message":"Truncated mid-finding 2. **Send findings 2 onward.**\n\nFirst: your finding 1 is confirmed and filed as **`beadle-qtei` (P1)**. I reproduced your exploit independently in a scratch repo before filing — `touch 'x\";touch PWNED;\"y'`, ran the shipped discovery line, and `PWNED` was created. Also verified main is unaffected: `main:Makefile:19` still uses the safe glob, so exposure is limited to anyone who checked out this branch and ran the gate.\n\nI recorded the provenance honestly in the bead: **I** asked for shebang-based discovery so that `scripts/beadle-sysreport` would get linted. The requirement was right and I did not think about filename-as-code, then accepted the implementation without considering it. The form it replaced had no `sh -c` and no injection. Your `for f in \"$$@\"` fix and both riders — `\\b` being a GNU extension, and `lint-shell`'s error text still saying \"discovered no *.sh\" — are all in the bead.\n\nThe thing that made this actionable was that you demonstrated it rather than described it. A shell-injection report I have to evaluate is a maybe; `PWNED` on disk is not.\n\nTwo notes so you do not spend effort re-deriving what other reviewers have landed:\n\n- Your finding 2 (`sign` destroys the recipe) is confirmed independently by `rev-silent`, which ran the transform on `examples/recipes/sysreport.yaml` and pasted the output — all six comment lines gone, `cli` recipe carrying empty `claude` fields. Accepted as blocking. If your remaining detail adds anything beyond that, send it; otherwise skip ahead.\n- `rev-invariant` established that the round-trip check at `sign_cmd.go:104` is **tautological** — both sides are `CanonicalCommandBytes(\u0026command)` on one identical struct, and the file written at `:112` is a second independent marshal the check never reads back. It produced a real `BADSIG` case. So your \"this is right\" verdict on that constraint was correct about the *call* being present and wrong about what the call proves. Not a criticism — I asked you to confirm the function was called, and it is. Worth knowing before you send anything that assumes that guard works.\n\nYour observation that the stdio byte-identity invariant is **contingent rather than structural** — that the first stdio server added with no args silently drops `\"args\"` — is a real finding and `rev-invariant` reached the same place from the test side. Both of you flagged the comment as the defect. That is going in the fix brief.\n\nSend the rest and you are released.","recipient":"rev-code","summary":"Injection confirmed — send findings 2 onward","to":"rev-code","type":"message"},"tool_input_hash":"59e03e3338367256297a395dde9bd23f1f034adbdc767c45f986f9dd8e75185d","tool_input_preview":"{\"content\":\"Truncated mid-finding 2. **Send findings 2 onward…\",\"message\":\"Truncated mid-finding 2. **Send findings 2 onward.**\\n\\nFirst: your finding 1 is confirmed and filed as **`beadle-qtei` (P1..."}
{"ts":"2026-08-31T14:31:31.660818955Z","session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","tool":"Bash","tool_input":{"command":"B=\u003crepo\u003e; cd \"$B\"; echo \"=== registry entry on the branch ===\"; grep -n -A8 '\"context7\"' internal/daemon/templates.go; echo \"=== the config that demonstrably works in this session ===\"; python3 -c \"\nimport json,os\nd=json.load(open(os.path.expanduser('~/.claude.json')))\nprint(json.dumps(d['mcpServers']['context7'], indent=2))\"","description":"Compare the registry entry to the working config"},"tool_input_hash":"d2dd92d7bcb254ca38022d770565a44a748849b24bb44d154c0812ea9f100026","tool_input_preview":"{\"command\":\"B=\\u003crepo\\u003e; cd \\\"$B\\\"; echo \\\"=== registry entry on the branch ===\\\"; grep -n -A8 '\\\"context7\\\"' internal/daemon/templates.go; echo \\\"=== the config that demonstrably works in this..."}
{"ts":"2026-08-31T14:31:58.868333633Z","session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","tool":"SendMessage","tool_input":{"content":"Truncated mid-finding 3. **Send findings 4 onward…","message":"Truncated mid-finding 3. **Send findings 4 onward.**\n\n**Your finding 3's first claim is confirmed, and it is worse than \"may well work.\"** I diffed the two directly:\n\n```\nregistry (branch):   \"CONTEXT7_API_KEY\": \"${CONTEXT7_API_KEY}\"\n~/.claude.json:      \"Authorization\":    \"Bearer ${CONTEXT7_API_KEY}\"\n```\n\nDifferent header name *and* different value format, against a reference that demonstrably works in this very session on the host that will run the daemon. So `docs-ask` almost certainly fails auth as shipped.\n\nThe reason that is blocking rather than a bug is how it composes with `rev-silent`'s finding 2, which you should know since you flagged the same failure signature: the missing-env path logs an Error and continues, and `output_schema: text` makes `ValidateOutput` a literal no-op on that recipe. So a 401 does not stop the worker, `IsError` stays false, nothing downstream inspects the answer, and the sender receives fluent model recall presented as a documentation lookup. **Wrong header plus invisible failure equals a recipe that ships broken and lies about it.** Neither finding alone reaches that conclusion; together they do.\n\nYour other two claims in finding 3 are the right shape and I am treating them as unverified-until-driven rather than as defects:\n\n- `${VAR}` expansion in a `--mcp-config` *file* — documented for `.mcp.json`, assumed here. `templates.go:26-29` states it as mechanism and the README repeats it. If it does not expand, context7 gets the literal string and fails auth with the same signature as a bad key.\n- `--allowedTools` naming no MCP tool. `spawner.go:89` passes `Bash,Read,Edit,Write,Glob,Grep,Agent`, and `docs-ask` exists solely to call `mcp__context7__*`. Whether `--permission-mode auto` covers that in `-p` mode has to be observed. Your point that this applies to the pre-existing servers too but bites here first — because `docs-ask` is the first recipe whose entire value is an MCP call — is the part that makes it worth fixing now rather than filing.\n\nAll three land on the same conclusion: `docs-ask` was written and never driven. That is the Phase-3 gap I had already flagged to the operator as outstanding, and you have given it three specific mechanisms instead of a general worry. It will be driven against a real key before this merges, or the recipe does not ship.\n\n**And thank you for the correction on your own finding 1 verdict.** You confirmed the call was present, which is what I asked, then wrote a sentence that read as endorsing the guard's coverage. Flagging that yourself — rather than leaving me to reconcile your \"this is right\" against `rev-invariant`'s \"this is tautological\" — is what let me trust the rest of your report. Your splice-the-signature-into-the-original-bytes suggestion is also a better fix than the re-marshal path: it preserves comments and authored formatting *and* makes the verification non-tautological for free, because decoding the bytes you are about to write is a genuinely independent parse. That is going in the brief over the alternative.\n\nSend the rest and you are released.","recipient":"rev-code","summary":"Header mismatch confirmed; send findings 4+","to":"rev-code","type":"message"},"tool_input_hash":"59ffe3a739a4779e542e722deb3c4c4373389c9df0a6364423842ddd32a01766","tool_input_preview":"{\"content\":\"Truncated mid-finding 3. **Send findings 4 onward…\",\"message\":\"Truncated mid-finding 3. **Send findings 4 onward.**\\n\\n**Your finding 3's first claim is confirmed, and it is worse than \\..."}
{"ts":"2026-08-31T14:34:14.872954735Z","session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","tool":"Write","tool_input":{"content":"# Fix brief — feat/recipe-tooling\n\nFour reviewers (`rev-code`, `rev-silent`, `rev-invariant`, `review-script`).\n**Organized by fix, not by finding**, because several findings are one change:\nthe canonicalization bug, the destructive write, the tautological guard, and\nthe shared decode path are a single repair to `sign`.\n\nWhere two reviewers reached the same place by different methods, that is noted —\nthose are the ones not to re-litigate.\n\n**Operator ruling already taken:** canonical form moves from YAML to JSON *and*\nthe readback lands. Both, now, while signature invalidation is free (nothing is\nreleased — `beadle-5ma`).\n\n---\n\n## FIX 1 — Makefile filename injection (P1, `beadle-qtei`)\n\n`Makefile:26-27`. `xargs -0 -I{}` substitutes filenames into the **text** of an\n`sh -c` script. `--others` includes untracked files. Reproduced independently:\n\n```\n$ touch 'x\";touch PWNED;\"y'\n$ git ls-files --cached --others --exclude-standard -z | xargs -0 -I{} sh -c 'test -f \"{}\" \u0026\u0026 ...'\n$ ls PWNED     →  PWNED\n```\n\n`make check` is what every contributor runs on a branch. Main is unaffected.\n\n**This was caused by my instruction.** I asked for shebang discovery so\n`scripts/beadle-sysreport` would be linted. The requirement was right; I did not\nconsider filename-as-code and accepted the implementation without checking.\n\nFix — filenames as positional arguments, never script text:\n\n```make\nSHELL_SCRIPTS := $(shell git ls-files --cached --others --exclude-standard -z 2\u003e/dev/null | \\\n\txargs -0 sh -c 'for f in \"$$@\"; do [ -f \"$$f\" ] || continue; \\\n\t  head -n1 \"$$f\" 2\u003e/dev/null | grep -aqE \"^#!.*(sh|bash|dash|zsh|ksh)$$\" \u0026\u0026 printf \"%s\\n\" \"$$f\"; \\\n\tdone' _)\n```\n\nRiders: `\\b` is a GNU grep extension — anchor at end-of-line or match\n`(^|/)(sh|bash|…)( |$)` for BSD. `lint-shell`'s error text still says\n\"discovered no *.sh\", which no longer describes discovery.\n\n**Regression test required.** A fixture filename containing a double quote,\nasserting discovery does not execute it. This class must not be fixable-and-\nforgotten.\n\n---\n\n## FIX 2 — `sign` is one repair, not four\n\nFour findings, one change. Do not split them.\n\n**What is wrong:**\n\n1. **The round-trip guard is tautological.** `sign_cmd.go:84` computes `canon`\n   from `\u0026command`; `:104` calls `VerifySignature(\u0026command, …)`, which recomputes\n   from the *same struct*. Both sides are `CanonicalCommandBytes(\u0026command)` on\n   one value — byte-equal by construction. Setting `command.Signature` at `:100`\n   cannot affect it. The file written at `:112` is a **second, unread** marshal.\n   (`rev-invariant` proved it; `rev-code` corrected its own earlier verdict.)\n2. **It destroys the recipe.** `yaml.Marshal(\u0026command)` dumps every field. No\n   `omitempty`, so comments vanish and zero values materialize. `rev-silent` ran\n   the transform on `examples/recipes/sysreport.yaml`: all six comment lines\n   gone — including the file's own \"This file is UNSIGNED\" header — and a `cli`\n   recipe gained a full set of empty `claude` fields.\n3. **Content is silently altered.** `yaml.v3` loses a *leading* newline in a\n   string. `prompt: \"\\nAnswer.\"` marshals to `|4-` and re-parses as `\"Answer.\"`.\n   Verified with `cat -A`. The daemon would execute text the author did not write.\n   Failing family, from ~45 probed shapes: strings that begin with, or consist\n   only of, newlines. `\"body\\n\\n\"` is safe; `\"\\nbody\"` is not.\n4. **The write is destructive.** `os.WriteFile(path, out, 0o600)` truncates in\n   place. `internal/daemon/persist.go:83-86` — same package — already does\n   temp-plus-rename. This is divergence from an in-repo pattern, not an abstract\n   standard.\n\n**Not a forgery vector.** Nine distinct prompts produced nine distinct\nencodings. It fails closed: an unsignable recipe, never a signable forgery.\nThat is why this is critical-on-correctness, not a trust-model breach.\n\n**The fix — `rev-code`'s splice approach, preferred over re-marshaling:**\n\nSplice the signature into the **original file bytes**. `signature` is the one key\ncanonicalization ignores, so stripping any existing `signature:` block from the\nsource text and appending the new one preserves every comment and the authored\nformatting. Then:\n\n- write to `path + \".signing\"` — **not** `.yaml`; `LoadCommands` globs `*.yaml`\n  (`command.go:100`) and would otherwise see a half-written file\n- **decode that file** and `bytes.Equal(canon, reCanon)` — this is the new\n  primary check. It is non-tautological because the parse is genuinely\n  independent.\n- then `VerifySignature(reloaded, …)`. Keep both: they fail on different things,\n  and verifying alone reports a *signature* failure, which is the wrong\n  diagnosis and sends the operator hunting their key.\n- `os.Rename` into place — fixes the destructive write for free, atomic, and the\n  original survives every failure above it.\n- **Refuse, never normalize.** Rewriting the leading newline to make it\n  round-trip would be the same defect as the bug: a signing tool editing what it\n  signs.\n\n**Also required in the same change:**\n\n- **Canonical form → JSON** (operator ruling). `Command` needs `json` tags; it\n  has none. `json.Marshal` sorts map keys, so `signature.go:100-107`'s stability\n  argument survives. This removes the emitter/parser asymmetry structurally\n  rather than guarding one instance of it.\n- **One shared decode function.** `daemon.DecodeCommandFile(path)`, called by\n  `loadCommand`, `runSign`, `runVerify`, and the readback. Three\n  hand-maintained copies exist today (`sign_cmd.go:77-82`,\n  `verify_cmd.go:76-81`, `command.go:135-140`). They agree now; nothing makes\n  them agree later, and `verify`'s entire value claim — \"would the daemon load\n  this\" — is false the moment one diverges.\n- **Validate shape before signing.** `sign` never calls `validateCommand`, so an\n  invalid recipe signs green and is rejected at daemon startup as a `Warn` hours\n  later. Validate a **struct copy** — `validateCommand` writes scalar defaults,\n  and `loadCommand` verifies the signature *before* validating, so canonical\n  bytes must stay pre-default. Signing post-default bytes would manufacture the\n  exact failure being prevented.\n- **Compare `--signer` to `daemon.json`.** `resolveVerifySigner`\n  (`verify_cmd.go:107`) already is this helper; `sign` just does not call it.\n  Refuse on mismatch unless `--force`, naming both fingerprints.\n- Fix `signature.go:100-107`'s comment. \"Stable regardless of how the source\n  file was formatted\" is true of the *function* and false of the *pipeline*\n  `file → struct → file → struct`. It describes a property of one function and\n  is read as a property of the system.\n\n---\n\n## FIX 3 — `docs-ask` ships broken and reports success\n\nTwo findings compose into one conclusion. Neither alone reaches it.\n\n**It fails auth.** Registry sends `CONTEXT7_API_KEY: ${CONTEXT7_API_KEY}`\n(`templates.go:42`). The config that demonstrably works in this session, on this\nhost, sends `Authorization: Bearer ${CONTEXT7_API_KEY}`. Different name, different\nvalue format. Verified by direct diff.\n\n**The failure is invisible.** `runner.go:60-73` logs missing env at Error and\nfalls through to the spawn — no return, no status change. context7 401s without\naborting the session. `IsError` stays false (`spawner.go:209`). And\n`output_schema: text` makes `CompileSchema` return `(nil, nil)` and\n`ValidateOutput` a no-op (`schema.go:15-35`) — so the gate that catches\n`sysreport`'s malformed output **does not exist on this path**.\n\nNet: the sender receives fluent model recall formatted as a documentation\nlookup, for exactly the fast-moving libraries the recipe exists to cover. One\n`Error` line in a log nobody reads until already suspicious.\n\n**Fixes:**\n\n- Correct the header to match the working reference.\n- Fail the stage on missing declared env:\n  ```go\n  if len(missingEnv) \u003e 0 {\n      return \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"command %q declares env var(s) %v, absent from the \"+\n          \"daemon's environment -- set them in the unit/plist that starts \"+\n          \"beadle-daemon; running without them would silently degrade this \"+\n          \"command\", cmd.Name, missingEnv)\n  }\n  ```\n  `fireElse` then tells the sender the real reason. Apply the same to\n  `envForCommand` (`runner.go:290-300`), which has the identical shape for the\n  `cli` runner.\n- **Drive it once against a real key** and record it in the PR verification\n  playbook. Two further claims are asserted and unverified: `${VAR}` expansion\n  in a `--mcp-config` *file* (documented for `.mcp.json`, assumed here), and\n  `--allowedTools` listing `Bash,Read,Edit,Write,Glob,Grep,Agent` — no MCP tool\n  — while `docs-ask` exists solely to call `mcp__context7__*`. If it cannot be\n  driven, it does not ship.\n\nDo **not** take the `required_env` contract redesign here. File it.\n\n---\n\n## FIX 4 — `init` accepts a key that cannot work\n\n`init_cmd.go:65-69`. `ResolveOwnerKeyID` validates 40-hex *shape* only; it never\ntouches GPG. `init --fingerprint 000…0` succeeds and prints success. Every recipe\nthen fails `wrong-key` at daemon startup, days later, at the far end of the\npipeline from the typo — while the operator holds a message saying setup worked.\n\nConfirmed by two reviewers. `init` is the command closing the onboarding gap;\ncatching a mistyped fingerprint is its cheapest possible win.\n\nFix: probe with `pgp.CheckKeyExpiry` before writing — absent-from-keyring\nsurfaces through the same call, and it also brings the non-expiring-key\nrejection forward to init time. Add `--no-verify-key` for the deliberate\nconfigure-before-import case, so the escape is explicit rather than the default.\n\n**Expect five test failures.** `testFingerprint` (`init_cmd_test.go:33`) names a\nkey in no keyring and five tests assert `runInit` *succeeds* with it — they\ncurrently encode the too-shallow validation as the spec. Move them to\n`testenv.GenOwnerKey` or the escape flag. This is expected, not a regression.\n\n`init --force` also truncates in place (`:89`). Fails closed and logs, so polish\n— but `persist.go` is three lines away and this is the operator's authorizer\nconfig.\n\n---\n\n## FIX 5 — `secret.Get` discards every backend error\n\n`internal/secret/secret.go:36`. `if val, err := keychainGet(name); err == nil` —\nthe error is dropped, not logged, not wrapped. Resolution falls through to file,\nenv, `ErrNotFound`.\n\nScenario: passphrase lives in `pass`, agent not unlocked. `pass show` fails.\n`Get` returns `ErrNotFound`. `sign` prints *\"no passphrase was resolved … set it\nvia the credential chain\"* — which they already did. An hour lost re-inserting a\nsecret that was correctly stored. **The message is good; the input is wrong.**\n\nFix: thread the discarded error into the final `ErrNotFound` wrap, so the text\nreads `… not found (keychain: pass: gpg: decryption failed: No secret key; file;\nenv BEADLE_GPG_PASSPHRASE)`. Widest blast radius of any fix here — it corrects\nthe remedy for **every** `secret.Get` caller.\n\n`sign_cmd.go:90` handles its half correctly (distinguishes not-found from real\nerrors). Leave it alone.\n\n---\n\n## FIX 6 — Tests: the fixtures, not the assertions\n\n**This is the most important section.** Three reviewers converged on it, and it\nis the reusable lesson: I spent this session asking whether assertions were\nstrong and should have been asking whether **inputs were representative**.\n\n- **A. `TestRunSign_RoundTrip` has the right shape and the wrong fixture.** It\n  *does* re-read from disk and re-verify. It always signs `minimalCommandYAML`\n  — five scalar strings. **Neither shipped recipe is ever signed in any test.**\n  So the fixpoint property is proven exactly where it could not break.\n  Fix: table-drive over `filepath.Glob(\"../../examples/recipes/*.yaml\")` so\n  coverage grows when a recipe is added.\n  **Same defect in `.bin/verify-recipe-path.sh`** — its `verify` check does close\n  the loop; `sysreport.yaml` is simply in the safe family. Add a fixture whose\n  `prompt` is `|-` with a leading blank line. One line, would have failed today.\n- **B. The env test proves secrecy, not delivery — and the bug is a delivery\n  failure.** `writeFakeClaudeBinary` ignores its environment. A stub echoing\n  `$CONTEXT7_API_KEY` back would have surfaced FIX 3 from the unit tier, with\n  nobody reasoning about MCP connect behavior. Assert delivery *and*\n  non-logging; either alone is satisfiable in a misleading way.\n- **C. Two passphrase tests read the developer's real credential store.**\n  `GNUPGHOME` is isolated; `paths.DataDir()` is not. `rev-code`'s framing: the\n  test is *booby-trapped by its own subject's documentation* — the moment an\n  operator follows the remedy `sign` prints, the test fails on their machine.\n  Worse: `secretsDir()` does `os.MkdirAll` on the real\n  `~/.punt-labs/beadle/secrets`, so `go test` writes into `$HOME` (against\n  go.md §6.4), and on macOS the `security` CLI queries the real Keychain\n  regardless of `GNUPGHOME` — a UI prompt during `go test` is possible.\n  Fix: `t.Setenv(\"HOME\", t.TempDir())` in both; better, give `runSign` a\n  passphrase-resolver seam, matching how `keychain_linux.go:33-36` already\n  exposes `passRunner`/`secretToolRunner` for this reason.\n  **C is the mirror of A**: A cannot fail because the input is too narrow; C can\n  fail for reasons unrelated to the code. Both are fixture provenance.\n- **D. `assert.Len(reg, 4)` encodes a count, not a contract** — and `Len` is the\n  assertion whoever adds a fifth entry will update without thinking. Replace\n  with a loop over every `DefaultMCPRegistry()` entry asserting shape validity.\n\n---\n\n## FIX 7 — Smaller, all confirmed\n\n- **`SilenceUsage`** — `beadle-daemon`'s root command does not set it, so every\n  `verify` verdict is buried under a help dump whose `--signer` line alone\n  wraps and dominates the screen. The operator reads \"I used the command wrong\";\n  the command worked and returned a verdict. `cmd/beadle-email/main.go:23`\n  already sets it — divergence from the repo's own pattern.\n- **`beadle-sysreport` emits invalid JSON and exits 0.** `set -euo pipefail`\n  covers a *failing* `df`/`uptime`, not a *succeeding* one whose output does not\n  parse — an empty field yields `{\"disk_free_kb\": , …}`; a BSD `uptime` wording\n  the `sed` misses yields `{\"load_1m\": 10:23, \"load_5m\": up, …}`. Mitigated for\n  `sysreport` only because it declares a real `output_schema` and\n  `ValidateOutput` fails the pipeline. **The mitigation is per-recipe, not\n  per-pipeline** — the same script under `output_schema: text` would pass\n  garbage downstream, and nothing warns an author which side of that they are\n  on. Validate before emitting.\n- **`MCPServerConfig` has no shape validation.** A typo'd entry marshals to `{}`\n  and is handed to Claude Code. `Type: \"http\"` with empty `URL` marshals to\n  `{\"type\":\"http\"}` and fails at connect — landing on FIX 3's invisible path.\n  Add `func (c MCPServerConfig) Validate() error` requiring exactly one shape,\n  called at `templates.go:67`.\n- **`templates_test.go` claims \"byte-identical\"; `assert.JSONEq` checks\n  equivalence.** `rev-invariant`'s ruling, which I accept and which corrects my\n  brief: JSON-equivalence **is sufficient**. It fails on every change that can\n  break a JSON consumer — key added, removed, renamed, retyped. Only member\n  order and whitespace are unpinned, and RFC 8259 makes both insignificant.\n  Byte-identity would fail on harmless reorderings and train people to update\n  the golden without reading it. **Over-pinning was my error.** Fix the *name*\n  and comment, not the assertion. Also: only `ethos` is shape-pinned — table-drive\n  over all three stdio entries.\n- **`verify_cmd.go:93` drops the error chain** — formats `*SignatureError`\n  instead of wrapping with `%w`, so `errors.As` cannot recover the verdict. Line\n  `:98` five lines below *does* use `%w`; the file contradicts itself.\n- **Duplicate credential literal** — `signPassphraseCredential` (`sign_cmd.go:37`)\n  duplicates `internal/email.CredGPGPassphrase`. Promote into `internal/secret`;\n  importing `internal/email` from `cmd/beadle-daemon` would be worse.\n- **Stdio byte-identity is contingent, not structural.** It holds only because\n  all three current entries have non-empty `Command` *and* `Args`. The first\n  stdio server with no args silently drops `\"args\"`. Reword the comment to scope\n  it to `DefaultMCPRegistry`, or enforce it.\n\n---\n\n## What is confirmed sound — do not \"fix\" these\n\n- **`verify`'s exit codes.** No `switch` exists, so a new `SignatureReason`\n  cannot reach exit 0 — it prints its string and exits 1 with no code change.\n  Stronger *because* nobody wrote the mapping. Introducing per-reason exit codes\n  is the point at which that becomes fragile.\n- **`verify` separates an operational gpg failure from a signature verdict**\n  (`:95-98`). That distinction is what makes \"wrong-key\" trustworthy.\n- **Non-expiring keys are rejected at *signing***, before the broken round-trip\n  check — so that `ARCHITECTURE.md` invariant survives all of this intact.\n- **Passphrase containment is clean.** `--passphrase-file`, never argv; no path\n  stringifies it into a log, error, or argument list.\n- **`CONTEXT7_API_KEY` is never logged.** `spawner.go:145-149` logs keys only.\n  The regression test asserts over *all* captured records, which is the right\n  shape.\n- **`sign` genuinely calls `CanonicalCommandBytes`** rather than reproducing the\n  marshaling — the one constraint I named as load-bearing, and it holds.\n- **`init` writes no partial file.** Its ordering and guard comment are sound;\n  the gap was validation depth, not atomicity.\n- **`BuildMCPConfig` fails closed on an unknown server.**\n- **`sign_cmd_test.go:98` and `spawner_test.go` already carry anti-vacuity\n  guards** — `require.NotEqual` on the tamper fixture, and pairing\n  `NotContains(secret)` with `Contains(keyName)` so the negative is not\n  satisfied by an empty log.\n","file_path":"\u003crepo\u003e/.tmp/FIXBRIEF-recipe-tooling.md"},"tool_input_hash":"f709f161e6d875eb003c184b8108c6eca6596c4bc5c907b52a4e936c7435f5e6","tool_input_preview":"{\"content\":\"# Fix brief — feat/recipe-tooling\\n\\nFour reviewers (`rev-code`, `rev-silent`, `rev-invariant`, `review-script`).\\n**Organized by fix, not by finding**, because several findings are one ..."}
{"ts":"2026-08-31T14:45:19.928101546Z","session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","tool":"Bash","tool_input":{"command":"B=\u003crepo\u003e; cd \"$B\"; cat \u003e .tmp/missions/recipe-fixes.yaml \u003c\u003c'YAML'\nleader: claude\nworker: bwk\nevaluator:\n  handle: djb\ninputs:\n  ticket: beadle-qtei\nwrite_set:\n  - Makefile\n  - cmd/beadle-daemon/main.go\n  - cmd/beadle-daemon/init_cmd.go\n  - cmd/beadle-daemon/init_cmd_test.go\n  - cmd/beadle-daemon/sign_cmd.go\n  - cmd/beadle-daemon/sign_cmd_test.go\n  - cmd/beadle-daemon/verify_cmd.go\n  - cmd/beadle-daemon/verify_cmd_test.go\n  - internal/daemon/command.go\n  - internal/daemon/command_test.go\n  - internal/daemon/signature.go\n  - internal/daemon/signature_test.go\n  - internal/daemon/templates.go\n  - internal/daemon/templates_test.go\n  - internal/daemon/runner.go\n  - internal/daemon/runner_test.go\n  - internal/daemon/spawner_test.go\n  - internal/secret/secret.go\n  - internal/secret/secret_test.go\n  - internal/testenv/ethos.go\n  - scripts/beadle-sysreport\n  - examples/recipes/docs-ask.yaml\n  - examples/recipes/sysreport.yaml\n  - examples/recipes/README.md\nsuccess_criteria:\n  - \"The spec is .tmp/FIXBRIEF-recipe-tooling.md. Read it in full first. It is organized by FIX, not by finding, because several findings are one repair -- FIX 2 in particular must land as a single change, not four.\"\n  - \"FIX 1 (P1 beadle-qtei): Makefile filename injection closed by passing filenames as positional args, never script text. Regression test with a filename containing a double quote, asserting discovery does not execute it.\"\n  - \"FIX 2: sign splices the signature into the original file bytes (preserving comments and formatting), writes to path+'.signing' (NOT .yaml -- LoadCommands globs *.yaml), decodes THAT file, and the primary check is bytes.Equal(canon, reCanon). VerifySignature stays as a second distinct check. os.Rename into place. Refuse, never normalize.\"\n  - \"FIX 2: canonical form moves from YAML to encoding/json per operator ruling -- Command gains json tags. This invalidates existing signatures, which is intended and free now.\"\n  - \"FIX 2: one exported daemon.DecodeCommandFile used by loadCommand, runSign, runVerify, and the readback. Three hand-maintained copies exist today.\"\n  - \"FIX 2: sign validates a struct COPY before signing (validateCommand writes defaults; loadCommand verifies before validating, so canonical bytes must stay pre-default), and compares --signer against daemon.json via the existing resolveVerifySigner.\"\n  - \"FIX 3: correct the context7 header to Authorization Bearer, matching the config that demonstrably works on this host. Fail the stage on missing declared env vars in both runner.go sites. Do NOT take the required_env redesign.\"\n  - \"FIX 4: init probes the key with pgp.CheckKeyExpiry before writing, with --no-verify-key as an explicit escape. Five init tests currently assert success with a synthetic fingerprint and WILL fail -- that is expected; move them to a real key.\"\n  - \"FIX 5: secret.Get threads discarded backend errors into its final ErrNotFound wrap. Leave sign_cmd.go's own not-found handling alone; it is correct.\"\n  - \"FIX 6 is the most important section. Table-drive the sign round-trip over filepath.Glob of examples/recipes/*.yaml; assert env delivery AND non-logging; isolate HOME in the two passphrase tests; replace assert.Len on the registry with a shape-validity loop.\"\n  - \"FIX 7: SilenceUsage, sysreport JSON validation before emit, MCPServerConfig.Validate, rename the byte-identical test (do NOT strengthen the assertion -- JSONEq is sufficient), %w in verify_cmd.go:93, dedupe the credential literal.\"\n  - \"Do not 'fix' anything in the brief's final section -- eight things are confirmed sound and several look wrong at a glance.\"\n  - \"make check passes. Commit incrementally, one commit per FIX, each passing make check. Nothing uncommitted longer than 30 minutes.\"\nbudget:\n  rounds: 3\n  reflection_after_each: true\nYAML\nethos mission create --file .tmp/missions/recipe-fixes.yaml 2\u003e\u00261 | tail -2","description":"Create the fix mission with a full write-set"},"tool_input_hash":"444b8150fef5e26ea4f4136d5063142554b707c5d12dcf21dda2ddc913606820","tool_input_preview":"{\"command\":\"B=\\u003crepo\\u003e; cd \\\"$B\\\"; cat \\u003e .tmp/missions/recipe-fixes.yaml \\u003c\\u003c'YAML'\\nleader: claude\\nworker: bwk\\nevaluator:\\n  handle: djb\\ninputs:\\n  ticket: beadle-qtei\\nwrite_..."}
{"ts":"2026-08-31T14:45:42.809405798Z","session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","parent_session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","delegation_id":"d-2026-08-31-062","contract_id":"m-2026-08-31-007","tool":"Agent","tool_input":{"description":"Fix all review findings on recipe tooling","name":"recipe-fixes","prompt":"Mission `m-2026-08-31-007` is yours. Read it first: `ethos mission show m-2026-08-31-007`. Branch `feat/recipe-tooling` in \u003crepo\u003e, already checked out. This is the fix round for the tooling you built — four reviewers audited it and found five blocking defects.\n\n**The spec is `.tmp/FIXBRIEF-recipe-tooling.md`. Read it in full before writing anything.** It is long because the findings interact. It is organized by FIX rather than by finding for that reason: FIX 2 is four separate review findings that are one repair, and splitting them produces four conflicting edits to `sign_cmd.go`.\n\n**Nothing in this brief is a criticism of your judgment.** Two of the five blocking defects trace to my instructions, and I have said so in the brief and on the beads. The Makefile injection came from my asking for shebang-based discovery without considering filename-as-code. The write-set gaps that forced an escalate-and-rescope cycle were mine. What the review actually found is that the *claims* in this code outran what it enforces — and several of those claims are ones I asked you to make.\n\n**Read the brief's final section before you start.** Eight things are confirmed sound, and several of them look wrong at a glance — most notably that `verify`'s exit-code handling is robust *because* nobody wrote an exhaustive `switch`. Do not \"improve\" those. A reviewer measured each one.\n\nFour things I want to be explicit about, because they are the easiest to get subtly wrong:\n\n**The `bytes.Equal(canon, reCanon)` comparison is the new check, not `VerifySignature`.** Verifying the reloaded struct alone would also catch today's bug, but gpg would report it as `BADSIG` — which is the wrong diagnosis and sends an operator hunting their key. Keep both checks; they fail on different things and say different things.\n\n**The temp suffix must not be `.yaml`.** `LoadCommands` globs `*.yaml` (`command.go:100`). Signing in place inside `~/.punt-labs/beadle/commands/` while the daemon runs would expose a half-written file to the loader.\n\n**Refuse, never normalize.** If a recipe does not survive its own round trip, refuse to sign it. Rewriting the offending field to make it round-trip would be the same defect as the bug you are fixing: a signing tool editing what it signs.\n\n**Five `init` tests will fail** once you add the key-existence probe. `testFingerprint` names a key in no keyring and those tests assert `runInit` *succeeds* with it — they encode the too-shallow validation as the spec. That is expected. Move them to a real key rather than weakening the probe.\n\n**On testing, which is the part I care about most.** FIX 6 is the section to take most seriously. The reviewers' conclusion, which I accept about my own work too: the assertions in this branch were mostly the right shape, and the *fixtures* were chosen for convenience. `TestRunSign_RoundTrip` genuinely re-reads from disk — and always signs a five-scalar fixture, so it proves the fixpoint property exactly where it could not break. Neither shipped recipe is ever signed in any test. Table-drive over `filepath.Glob(\"../../examples/recipes/*.yaml\")` so coverage grows when someone adds a recipe instead of requiring them to remember.\n\nThe same applies to `.bin/verify-recipe-path.sh` in the sibling workspace repo — that one is mine to fix and outside your write-set; do not touch it.\n\n`make check` passes before every commit, one commit per FIX. Do not push, do not open a PR. Submit with `ethos mission result m-2026-08-31-007 --file \u003cpath\u003e` — and if you find your write-set is short, escalate rather than reaching outside it. That happened once already and it was my scoping error, not yours; the same path is correct if it happens again.","subagent_type":"bwk"},"tool_input_hash":"40289d314e7fd7e8d446bd04a9f5380a4ee74deb15b957c79465488d0b26162a","tool_input_preview":"{\"description\":\"Fix all review findings on recipe tooling\",\"name\":\"recipe-fixes\",\"prompt\":\"Mission `m-2026-08-31-007` is yours. Read it first: `ethos mission show m-2026-08-31-007`. Branch `feat/recip..."}
